{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block body_class %}user-guide{% endblock %}
{% block title %}User Guide – DHCP Management{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'DHCP Management' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network me-2"></i>DHCP Management</h1>
    <a href="{{ path('servers_index') }}" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle me-1"></i>Go to DHCP Servers
    </a>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

<div class="card mb-4" id="overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-info-circle me-1"></i>Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI generates <a href="https://kea.isc.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kea DHCP</a> configuration from your subnet and host data, then deploys it to your DHCP servers via SSH. It also receives lease notifications from Kea via a webhook so you can track current and historical lease activity.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">The flow is: <strong>Subnets &amp; hosts</strong> → <strong>Config generation</strong> → <strong>SSH deploy</strong> → <strong>Kea reload</strong> → <strong>Lease notifications back to DashDDI</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="dhcp-servers">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-server me-1"></i>DHCP Servers</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Register your Kea DHCP servers at <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (DHCP section). Each server needs:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name <span class="text-danger">*</span></dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">A label for this server, e.g. <code>kea-primary</code>.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Hostname / IP</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The IP address or hostname DashDDI uses to SSH into this server.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH User</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The Linux user account for the SSH connection (default: <code>root</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH Private Key</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Generated automatically by DashDDI. Copy the displayed public key to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> on the Kea server.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Remote Path</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Directory on the server where DashDDI writes the generated Kea config files (default: <code>/etc/kea</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Control Port</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">TCP port the Kea Control Agent listens on (default: <code>8000</code>). DashDDI uses this to trigger a config reload over the SSH session — the agent does not need to be exposed externally.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">DDNS Enabled</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">When checked, DashDDI generates and deploys a <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> file for Kea's D2 daemon alongside the regular subnet files. Requires DDNS to be configured on at least one DNS server and domain (see the DNS guide).</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-muted small">For SSH key installation, generated config file layout, and Kea D2 setup, see the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_servers') }}#dhcp-integration">Servers &amp; Integrations guide → DHCP / Kea</a>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="config-generation">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-file-earmark-code me-1"></i>Configuration Generation</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI generates Kea DHCP4 configuration by combining data from:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Subnets</strong> assigned to the DHCP server (via the subnet's DHCP server field), including their gateway, DNS views, and address blocks</li>
            <li><strong>Host interfaces</strong> — any interface with a MAC address and a static IP gets a <code>host reservation</code> entry</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The generated config includes:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>A <code>subnet4</code> block for each subnet (CIDR, gateway, pools derived from address blocks)</li>
            <li>A <code>reservations</code> section with one entry per host interface that has a static IP</li>
            <li>Global options for lease time, DNS servers, and domain name</li>
        </ul>

        <h6 class="mt-4">DHCPv6</h6>
        <p>DHCPv6 clients don't send a MAC address, so <code>subnet6</code> reservations are matched on <strong>DUID</strong> instead when the host has one set (see the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_hosts') }}#interfaces">Hosts guide</a> for where to set it). Interfaces on a host without a DUID fall back to MAC-address-based reservations, same as DHCP4.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">Kea matches an incoming, non-relayed (link-local) DHCPv6 request to a subnet by the interface it arrived on, not by a relay address. Set <strong>DHCPv6 On-Net Interface</strong> on the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_subnets') }}#create-subnet">subnet</a> that's directly attached (on-link) to the DHCP server to emit that subnet's <code>interface</code> key in <code>subnets6.json</code>; leave it blank for subnets only reached through a relay agent. The base <code>kea-dhcp6.conf</code>'s <code>interfaces-config</code> section (which interfaces Kea actually binds/listens on) is maintained directly on the Kea server, outside DashDDI.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="push-dhcp">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-cloud-upload me-1"></i>Pushing DHCP Configuration</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Push the generated config to your Kea server in two ways:</p>
        <ol class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Manually</strong> — navigate to the DHCP server in <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> and click <strong>Push Config</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Scheduled</strong> — set up a cron-based push in <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0">The push uploads the config file to the configured path on the DHCP server, then runs the reload command. Execution output is visible in <strong>Servers → Push Logs</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="ddns">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-arrow-repeat me-1"></i>Dynamic DNS (DDNS)</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>When DDNS is enabled, Kea's D2 daemon automatically registers hostnames in BIND as leases are granted, keeping forward and reverse DNS in sync with DHCP allocations.</p>
        <p>To enable DDNS on a DHCP server:</p>
        <ol class="mb-2">
            <li>Configure a <strong>TSIG key</strong> (algorithm + secret) on the target DNS server.</li>
            <li>Enable <strong>DDNS</strong> on a domain and point it at that DNS server.</li>
            <li>Set <strong>DDNS Domain</strong> on each subnet whose reverse zone should receive dynamic updates.</li>
            <li>Check <strong>DDNS Enabled</strong> on this DHCP server and push config.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>DashDDI generates <code>kea-dhcp-ddns.conf</code> from the configured domains and subnets and deploys it alongside the regular subnet files. The file contains TSIG key definitions and forward/reverse zone entries for the Kea D2 daemon.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">The Kea D2 daemon is a separate package (<code>kea-dhcp-ddns-server</code>) and requires a matching entry in <code>kea-ctrl-agent.conf</code>. See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_servers') }}#kea-ddns">Servers &amp; Integrations guide → DHCP / Kea → Dynamic DNS</a> for full setup details.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="leases">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-journal-text me-1"></i>DHCP Leases</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Browse active and historical DHCP leases at <strong>DHCP → DHCP Leases</strong>. When Kea is configured to notify DashDDI (see below), lease records are stored automatically whenever a lease is granted or renewed.</p>
        <p>The lease list shows:</p>
        <ul class="mb-2">
            <li>IP address, MAC address, hostname reported by the client</li>
            <li>Subnet the lease belongs to</li>
            <li>Lease start and expiry times</li>
            <li>Whether the lease is currently active</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-2">The host list and host detail pages show the <strong>current active lease</strong> for each interface's MAC address alongside any static IP — making it easy to see if a device is getting the expected address.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">To enable lease tracking, configure Kea to post lease events to DashDDI using the <code>run_script</code> hook — see <a href="{{ path('user_guide_servers') }}#kea-lease-notifications">Kea lease notifications</a> in the Servers &amp; Integrations guide.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="clearpass-auth-logs">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>ClearPass Authentication Logs</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>If ClearPass is configured, authentication events are available at <strong>DHCP → ClearPass Auth Logs</strong>. Each entry shows the MAC address, username, IP address, and authentication outcome for network access requests.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_servers') }}#clearpass">Servers &amp; Integrations guide → ClearPass</a> for setup details.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="subnet-change-report">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-arrow-left-right me-1"></i>Subnet Change Report</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The <a href="{{ path('report_subnet_change') }}">Subnet Change Report</a> (under <strong>Reports</strong> in the nav) compares each device's last DHCP lease before a cutover time to its most recent lease after, highlighting any that landed in a different subnet.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and time of your cutover in the <strong>Cutover Date/Time</strong> field and click <strong>Run Report</strong>. The table shows:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Pre-cutover Subnet / VLAN</strong> — the subnet and VLAN the device was in immediately before the cutover.</li>
            <li><strong>Post-cutover Subnet / VLAN</strong> — the subnet and VLAN it is in now (most recent lease).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Only devices that have DHCP lease records on both sides of the cutover time are included. Devices with no post-cutover lease (not yet re-connected) will not appear.</p>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /col-lg-9 #}

<div class="col-lg-3 d-none d-lg-block">
    <div class="card sticky-top" style="top:80px">
        <div class="card-header small fw-semibold">On this page</div>
        <div class="list-group list-group-flush small">
            <a href="#overview" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Overview</a>
            <a href="#dhcp-servers" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">DHCP Servers</a>
            <a href="#config-generation" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Config Generation</a>
            <a href="#push-dhcp" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Pushing Config</a>
            <a href="#ddns" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Dynamic DNS (DDNS)</a>
            <a href="#leases" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">DHCP Leases</a>
            <a href="#clearpass-auth-logs" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Auth Logs</a>
            <a href="#subnet-change-report" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Subnet Change Report</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /row #}
{% endblock %}
